						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3054.msg35502#msg35502">Quote from: Budaho on August 13, 2007, 11:50:44 am</a></div><div class="quote"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3054.msg35460#msg35460">Quote from: antigrl on August 11, 2007, 11:07:05 pm</a></div><div class="quote">I want to see it!<br /><br />Last school year when I was a teacher at a school for Korean students, I had a conversation with my boss about American vs. Korean schools.  He talked a lot about how in Korea, you can&#039;t just slide by...that you have to compete and work hard because there are so few jobs and so many people.  At the Chinese school I work at on Saturdays, the parents push their children hard (even though they are in American schools).  I had 3rd graders who were reading books at middle school levels and they were in a Saturday morning class to improve their reading and writing.<br /><br />In general, from what I&#039;ve seen in my teaching experience...American parents are afraid/don&#039;t want to put pressure on their kids to study hard...giving kids time to be kids and have free time is a total priority.  We have parents who complain when their kids get homework and then give the teachers excuses as to why their child can&#039;t do the homework.  <br /><br />This summer, at the summer camp I&#039;m working at, I&#039;m seeing that a lot of American kids are lazy and their parents enable it.  These kids come to the camp every single day complaining about how it&#039;s their summer and they shouldn&#039;t have to learn anything at all (the teachers have to create the curriculum, and it&#039;s mostly arts and crafts based...).  There is an increasing desire for teachers to dress up in clown costumes and dance around like monkeys to entertain the kids.<br /><br />Okay...I&#039;m going to stop ranting now haha.<br /></div><br />i couldn&#039;t agree with this more, and its deffinately not limited to camps, this is one of the major problems with american schooling, and more and more with our society in general.  many many parents completely cop out when it comes to taking responsibility for teaching their kids anything, from social skills, to discipline, to whats generally concidered education.  they expect the schools to do it all, and its an expectation that won&#039;t ever be realized.  thats the main reason you often do see asian american children doing so much better in school, often the cultures they come from encourage the opposite attitude.  and the extra support and push that creates in the home environment  makes an extrodinary difference.  i think its kind of the elephant in the room nobody wants to mention though.  i think teachers administrators and politicians in general are just scared to go to their bosses (parents, ie elecotrate) and tell them they need to take responsibility for this themselves.  telling voters that they need to change their personal and/or family lives in any way is probably one of the biggest political taboos there is.  even though i think alot of families, especially lower income ones, are looking for both the guidance and the support they need to provide the home support kids really do need to learn.<br /></div><br />TRUTH!</td>
						</tr>
